 "eras": [
  {"id":"1948","year":1948,"label":"Before the 1969 floods","short":"1948","lens":"none","summary":"Matmata as a Berber pit-dwelling village before the floods of 1969 and the move to the new surface town: courtyard houses dug into the hillsides, rooms on two levels around each pit.","basis":"The 1948 Bulletin (MAT-06) describes the pits (7-9 m deep, a sloping passage, rooms on two levels) and counts the dwellings and people. The 26 drawn pit rims are the present OpenStreetMap rims carried back, and the wadis, cemetery and the three zaouias likewise.","conjecture":"No plan, aerial photograph or survey of any date was found for any pit. Which of the 26 mapped pits existed in 1948, and where, is not known: many were rebuilt or collapsed after 1969, so the carried-back rims are the weakest claim in the scene. Modern roads, the hotel, shops and about 200 mapped buildings are removed because their age is unknown. Depth is inferred (8 m); no room is drawn.","sources":["MAT-01","MAT-02","MAT-03","MAT-06","MAT-09"],"facts":["Bulletin 1948 (MAT-06): a central excavation 7 to 9 m deep, a sloping access passage dug from the hillside, sometimes very long and vaulted, and cavities on two levels; the visited house had three main rooms and alcoves beside the tunnel to be enlarged when children marry. The OCR reads about 700 dwellings and 4,000-5,000 people; the figure is uncertain.","Fabrizi (MAT-01): courtyards 5-10 m across and no deeper than 9 m; two floors (sleeping rooms, kitchen and stables below, storage above); access by a cut stair, a ladder or a graded tunnel.","Wikipedia (MAT-03): rain for 22 days in 1969 flooded and collapsed many pit homes; most people rebuilt underground and only a few families moved to the new surface town."]},
  {"id":"1976","year":1976,"label":"The Star Wars shoot","short":"1976","lens":"none","summary":"Hotel Sidi Driss, five pits joined by lateral tunnels since 1967-68, is the Lars homestead interior: George Lucas films the dining-room set in pit 2 on 1 April 1976.","basis":"MAT-04 gives the hotel (1968), its five pits and four tunnels, the pit-2 coordinate used for the marker, and the 1976 dates; MAT-03 and MAT-05 confirm the Star Wars use. The pit rims are the present OSM rims carried back.","conjecture":"Only pit 2 has a published coordinate and only to an unstated accuracy; the other four hotel pits are four nearby mapped rims chosen by distance (a fifth is not identified), so the five-pit cluster is assumed. The 1976 sets are not drawn: set construction started in mid-February 1976 but the sources give no change to the pits, and the Lars exterior was a separate set on the Chott el-Djerid, 265 km west. Everything else is carried back as in 1948.","sources":["MAT-03","MAT-04","MAT-05","MAT-08"],"facts":["Hotel Sidi Idriss was founded in 1968; its five pits and four lateral tunnels were built in the late 1960s (MAT-04).","The dining room (pit 2, cave 9) was filmed on 1 April 1976 (scenes A28 and A28A, the latter later deleted); the kitchen was built at EMI-Elstree on the pit 1-2 tunnel and steps as template (MAT-04).","Atlas Obscura (MAT-05): residents began leaving in the 1960s and 1970s under the modernisation drive; Hedi Ali Kayel dug the last new house in the 1970s (Al Jazeera, MAT-07)."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"Tourist village and hotel","short":"Present","lens":"none","summary":"Matmata el Qadima today: some pit houses still lived in, some abandoned, some converted to hotels, cafes and shops; the surface town and road lie around the old pits.","basis":"Pit rims, roads, buildings, walls, zones and wadis are OpenStreetMap (ODbL); the ground is Terrarium; the Sidi Driss marker is the MAT-04 coordinate.","conjecture":"Every pit depth is inferred (8 m), every building and wall without a height is kerb-high, and no room, stair or door of any pit is drawn. Whether each mapped pit is still open, collapsed or built over was not checked. The ruined Kalaa above the village is a separate row (matmata-kalaa-n11034987883) and lies outside this window.","sources":["MAT-01","MAT-02","MAT-03","MAT-04","MAT-05","MAT-07","MAT-08"],"facts":["Population 2,116 in 2004 (MAT-03); tourism fell after 2011 and 2015 (MAT-05, MAT-07).","Old Matmata uses a mixed vertical and horizontal excavation, and the interior stays under 27 C in summer (MAT-09); about 90% of rustic troglodyte works are abandoned, 10% ruined (MAT-09).","Some families built modern houses beside the old ones and use the pits as stables, workshops or warehouses (MAT-07)."]}
 ],
